---
title: Testing
summary: The devtools and the profiler as test primitives. Your Playwright tests measure hydration, interactions, server time and weight, and fail on a budget.
related:
  - devtools/overview
  - profiler/overview
---

`ogygia/testing` turns the devtools and the profiler into primitives for your own tests. Your tests click through the app as they always do. ogygia measures what happened, and your test asserts on it.

- `page()`: the page load as the browser measured it. Vitals, each island's wake, and the findings of the [Page tab](/docs/devtools/overview#the-page-tab).
- `hydration()`: each island's hydration status, with the difference for a changed one (the [Hydration tab](/docs/devtools/overview#the-hydration-tab)).
- `record(actions)`: what your actions did. Slow interactions and the function behind each, dead and repeated clicks, requests with the server's own split, long tasks, shifts, errors (the [Record tab](/docs/devtools/overview#recording-a-session)).
- `profile(path)`: the [SSR profiler](/docs/profiler/overview)'s report of a path. Score, forecast, findings, and weight.

## With Playwright

ogygia does not import Playwright. You hand it your own `test` and `expect`:

```ts title=tests/fixtures.ts
import { test as base, expect as baseExpect } from '@playwright/test';
import { withOgygia } from 'ogygia/testing';

export const { test, expect } = withOgygia(base, baseExpect);
```

```ts title=tests/checkout.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';

test('checkout stays fast', async ({ page, ogygia }) => {
  await page.goto('/cart');

  expect(await ogygia.page()).toMeetBudget({ lcp: 2500, cls: 0.1 });
  expect(await ogygia.hydration()).toHydrateCleanly();

  const session = await ogygia.record(async () => {
    await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Buy' }).click();
  });
  expect(session).toHaveNoFindings({ severity: 'warn' });
  expect(session).toMeetBudget({ maxInteractionMs: 200, errors: 0 });

  expect(await ogygia.profile('/cart')).toMeetBudget({ score: 80, renderMs: 200 });
});
```

The server under test needs `ogygia({ devtools: true })`: the dev server, or a preview build with devtools on (see [Devtools in a build](/docs/devtools/overview#enabling-in-a-build-a-preview-deploy)). The fixture turns devtools on for your `baseURL` (the `og_devtools` cookie), or with no `baseURL` for the origin of the first page the test opens, so each page is measured from its first load. `profile()` needs `ogygia({ profiler: true })`. On a build, give it the profiler's key:

```ts title=playwright.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  use: { baseURL: 'http://127.0.0.1:4173', ogygiaOptions: { profilerKey: process.env.OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET } },
});
```

A recording can span pages: actions that do a full page load (`page.goto`, a form post, a plain link) keep recording on the next page, and the report covers all of them (`session.counts.pages`). If the actions leave for a document with no testing API (another site, a page without devtools), the recording is lost and `record()` says so, with your actions' own error as its `cause`.

To keep the page instead, record with `blockNavigation`. Every document load the actions cause (a reload in a handler, a stray link) is answered `204`, so the browser stays where it is, and each one is listed:

```ts
const session = await ogygia.record(() => page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Filter' }).click(), { blockNavigation: true });
session.navigations; // [{ url: 'https://…/products', after: 'button.filter "Filter"', island: 'ProductFilter', t: 4120, approx: false }]
```

`after` is what started the navigation, seen as it began: the link or form, or the input being handled then (a reload inside a click handler names that button). Where the browser cannot say (no Navigation API, which is Chromium-only) or the site runs an older engine, `approx` is `true` and `after` is only the last input before it. `navigations` is the list to count and compare. Each entry is also a `navigation-blocked` finding, for reading, which `compare` leaves out.

Each island in `hydration()` has a `key` for its instance, so a diff can say which carousel broke: `Carousel#2`, the second island of that name in page order. That number moves when an island is added earlier on the page. For a key that survives that, give the fixture `ogygiaOptions: { islandKey: 'data-block-id' }`: each island is keyed by that attribute on itself or its nearest ancestor that has it, across shadow roots (an island inside a web component finds the block around the component). Two islands under the same keyed element are `id` and `id#2`. Session findings carry `names`, the islands they point at, like page findings.

### When the site runs an older ogygia

The reports are built by the page's testing engine, which ships with the site's build, not with your test's `ogygia` package. A check that measures an older deploy (a baseline) next to a new preview gets older reports from it. Every report says which engine answered (`report.engine.version`; 2 is current), and the first older one logs a warning. What the test side knows is filled in: `navigations` from the loads it held back (with `approx: true`), `names: []` on findings. Instance keys and `counts.cls` are absent there, and `compare` then tallies hydration by name and skips the session's CLS rather than report a false move.

## The server, over the same actions

`record(actions, { server: true })` also records the server while your actions run: the profiler's CPU across every request they made, their outbound calls, and the requests themselves. It lands on the session as `server`:

```ts
const session = await ogygia.record(() => page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Buy' }).click(), { server: true });

session.server.window.requests; // [{ method: 'POST', path: '/cart/checkout', ms: 412, status: 200 }, …]
expect(session.server).toHaveNoFindings({ severity: 'warn' });
expect(session).toMeetBudget({ serverBusyMs: 300 });
```

Under the hood this is the profiler's window: `POST /__profiler/window/start`, then `POST /__profiler/window/stop` answers the report. One window runs at a time. It waits a few seconds for a background sample to finish, and stops itself after a minute.

## The matchers

| Matcher | Passes when |
| --- | --- |
| `toHaveNoFindings({ severity?, ignore? })` | No finding at or above `severity` (`info`, `warn`, `error`), except the codes in `ignore`. Works on page, session and profile reports. |
| `toMeetBudget(budget)` | Every line of the budget is met. |
| `toHydrateCleanly({ allow? })` | No island failed, recovered, or changed its markup (`allow` lets some statuses through). |

A failing matcher says what missed and where: `Clock: changed — −rendered on the server +rendered on the browser`.

Budget lines, by report:

| Report | Lines |
| --- | --- |
| `page()` | `lcp`, `fcp`, `cls`, `inp`, `ttfb`, `longTaskMs`, `islandWakeMs` (the slowest island's wake) |
| `record()` | `maxInteractionMs`, `slowInteractions`, `errors`, `cls` (shifts with no input), `longTaskMs`, `failedRequests`, `serverBusyMs` (with `server: true`) |
| `profile()` | `score` (at least), `renderMs` (the median render), `jsBytes` (JS at start; a build only) |

`checkBudget(report, budget)` returns the same lines as data, for your own logic.

## Reports in CI

Every report a test takes is attached to its result as `ogygia.json`. Playwright's HTML reporter shows it, and the JSON reporter carries it. Two helpers turn two runs (a base branch and a head branch) into a PR comment:

```ts title=scripts/ogygia-comment.ts
import { compare, toMarkdown } from 'ogygia/testing';

// each run: the parsed `ogygia.json` attachments ({ test, reports }[])
const diff = compare(baseRun, headRun);
console.log(toMarkdown(diff)); // what moved, per test
```

Per test, the diff carries:

- **Findings:** new and fixed, by code.
- **Profiles:** score, server render and JS at start, with the files behind a JS move.
- **Hydration:** each island instance that stopped or started hydrating cleanly (`Carousel#2: clean → failed`).
- **Sessions:** the counts that moved (errors, unexpected shifts, slow interactions, blocked navigations, the session's CLS).
- **Vitals:** only those that moved past a run's noise. Defaults: LCP and FCP 150 ms, TTFB 100 ms, INP 50 ms (each also 10 % of the base), CLS 0.02. Set your own with `compare(baseRun, headRun, { noise: { lcp: 300 } })`.

### Several runs a side

Real pages are noisy, and one run against one run turns that noise into false regressions: a scripted session's errors, failed requests and interaction shifts depend on the path it happened to take. Give a test several runs a side, `{ test, runs: [reports, reports, reports] }`, and `compare` splits the signals:

- **Load signals** (page vitals and findings, hydration, the profile's score, render time and JS) are compared on the runs' median. A finding or a broken island counts when most runs have it.
- **Session signals** (its findings, errors, shifts, slow interactions, blocked navigations) count only when every head run has them and no base run does. A count moves only when every head run is above every base run, or below.

Three runs a side is a good start, for the pages whose first pass shows a candidate.

## Other runners and drivers

`createOgygia(driver)` gives the same four primitives over any driver that can run a function in the page (`evaluate`) and, for `profile()`, make a request (`get`). Puppeteer's `Page` fits. The matchers are plain `expect.extend` matchers:

```ts
import { expect } from 'vitest';
import { createOgygia, ogygiaMatchers } from 'ogygia/testing';

expect.extend(ogygiaMatchers);
const ogygia = createOgygia({ evaluate: (fn, arg) => page.evaluate(fn, arg) });
```

Holding Playwright pages yourself (two browser contexts in one test, say a preview and a baseline) without the fixture? `playwrightDriver(page)` is the driver the fixture builds, `blockNavigation` included, and `turnOnDevtools(page, baseURL)` sets the cookie. `onNavigationHeld` is called as each load is held back, so you can note which step of your script was running:

```ts
import { createOgygia, playwrightDriver, turnOnDevtools } from 'ogygia/testing';

await turnOnDevtools(previewPage, previewURL);
const preview = createOgygia(playwrightDriver(previewPage, { baseURL: previewURL, onNavigationHeld: (n) => log(step, n.url) }));
```
